    {
      "id": 11,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "How to read this dashboard",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 6,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 18
      },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "code": {
          "language": "plaintext",
          "showLineNumbers": false
        },
        "content": "### The three numbers that decide whether to act\n\n| Panel | Question it answers | Act when |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Availability** | Are users getting errors? | below 99.5% |\n| **Error budget remaining** | How much room is left this month? | below 25% |\n| **Burn rate** | At this speed, will we run out? | above 1.0 |\n\n### Where to go next\n\n* Alerts and their thresholds: **Prometheus \u2192 /alerts** (rules are generated from the SLO\n  definition by `scripts/render_rules.py`).\n* Per-endpoint latency and status breakdown: the **API & Latency Detail** dashboard.\n* What to actually do: **`docs/runbooks/`** \u2014 every alert annotation links to one.\n* The objective itself and the policy when it is missed: **`docs/SLO.md`**.\n\n### Why probe traffic is excluded everywhere\n\n`/healthz/`, `/readyz/`, `/metrics` and `/version/` are generated by monitoring on a fixed\nschedule. Counting them would let a probe loop that never fails drag the measured error rate\ntowards zero, no matter what real users experienced.\n"
      }
    },
